The reviewers generally agreed that this paper tackles an important and underexplored problem: reconstructing high-fidelity 3D clothed humans from unconstrained photo collections, with strong quantitative and qualitative performance, and a favorable efficiency-quality trade-off compared to prior methods.

The main concerns informing the initial mixed scores were:
- missing ablations for key design choices (image encoder in PCFA, backbone of the shape predictor); 
- insufficient comparisons with standard human reconstruction baselines (e.g., ICON, ECON, PIFuHD); 
- limited robustness analysis under challenging conditions such as loose clothing, extreme poses, high occlusion, and missing body parts;
- questions about novelty and efficiency attribution, given the reliance on existing components (MV-Adapter, PSHuman).

The rebuttal and discussion phase substantially strengthened the paper through new experiments, analyses, and clarifications, addressing most of these concerns. Considering the paper's effectiveness and efficiency, AC recommends acceptance.
